data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc`'d `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`.** It works just like any other unmanaged type:

```csharp
Span<nint> span = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> uspan = stackalloc nuint[2];
```

On this 64-bit system, `nint` is 8 bytes, so `nint.MaxValue` is `9223372036854775807` (same as `long.MaxValue`). The native-sized integer types (`nint`/`nuint`) satisfy the `unmanaged` constraint required by `stackalloc`, so no special flags are needed — just standard safe code.
